(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-premalignant-and-malignant-lesions-quiz"]={"title":"Pre-Malignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"The Clark level diagram maps invasion against skin anatomy. Which layers do levels I through V correspond to?","choices":["I into the papillary dermis; II into the reticular dermis; III into subcutaneous tissue; IV into muscle; V into bone","I confined to the stratum corneum; II into the epidermis; III into the papillary dermis; IV into the reticular dermis; V into the dermis-subcutaneous junction","I confined to the epidermis; II into the papillary dermis; III filling the papillary dermis; IV into the reticular dermis; V into the subcutaneous tissue","I confined to the epidermis; II into the reticular dermis; III filling the reticular dermis; IV into the papillary dermis; V into the subcutaneous tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — Clark level is an ANATOMIC level, unlike Breslow thickness which is a measurement. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How does Clark level differ from Breslow thickness, and which does the deck call the dominant prognostic variable?","choices":["Clark level is the anatomic layer reached; Breslow thickness is a measured depth, and Breslow is the dominant prognostic variable","Clark level is a measured depth; Breslow thickness is the anatomic layer reached, and Clark is the dominant prognostic variable","Both describe the same measurement on different scales, and either may be used as the dominant prognostic variable","Clark level is the anatomic layer reached; Breslow thickness is a measured depth, and Clark is the dominant prognostic variable"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — Breslow is measured in millimetres and drives margins and staging. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"In the TNM melanoma staging overview, what do the three letters denote?","choices":["T is primary tumour thickness; N is the number of tumour-involved regional lymph nodes; M is the number of metastases at a distant site","T is the tumour's anatomic level; N is the number of naevi present; M is the number of mitoses per high-power field","T is the time since onset; N is the number of tumour-involved regional lymph nodes; M is the number of distant metastases","T is primary tumour thickness; N is the nodal diameter in centimetres; M is the mitotic rate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the footnote of the staging table defines all three. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which TNM combination defines stage 0 melanoma?","choices":["Tis, N1a, M0","T0, N0, M1","Tis, N0, M0","T1a, N0, M0"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — carcinoma in situ with no nodal or distant disease. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What defines stage IV melanoma in the staging overview?","choices":["Any T, any N, with M1 — that is, distant metastasis present whatever the primary or nodal status","T4b, N3a/b/c, with M0 — the deepest primary with extensive nodal disease but no distant spread","Any T with N0 and M0 — a thick primary with no nodal or distant disease","Tis with any N and any M — in situ disease with any degree of spread"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — M1 alone determines it. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"In the Stages of Melanoma diagram, what distinguishes stage III from stage IV?","choices":["Stage III is melanoma confined to the epidermis; stage IV is localised disease in the skin","Stage III is spread to the lymph nodes; stage IV is spread to other organs","Stage III is spread to other organs; stage IV is spread to the lymph nodes","Stage III is localised disease thicker than stage II; stage IV is spread to the lymph nodes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — nodal against distant spread. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"In the Stages of Melanoma diagram, what are stage 0, stage I and stage II?","choices":["Stage 0 is localised disease only in the skin and very thin; stage I is melanoma confined to the epidermal region; stage II is disease that has spread to the lymph nodes","Stage 0 is disease that has spread to the lymph nodes; stage I is melanoma confined to the epidermal region; stage II is localised disease that is very thin","Stage 0 is localised disease thicker than stage I; stage I is melanoma confined to the epidermal region; stage II is disease that has spread to other organs","Stage 0 is melanoma confined to the epidermal region of the skin; stage I is localised disease, only in skin and very thin; stage II is localised disease thicker than stage I"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — 0, I and II are all still local. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Where do actinic keratoses occur, and what does finding several in one anatomic zone imply?","choices":["Sun-exposed face, scalp, ears, forearms and dorsal hands; multiple lesions in one zone suggest field cancerization and favour field-directed therapy","Sun-protected trunk, axillae and groin; multiple lesions in one zone suggest an immunosuppressed host and favour systemic therapy","Palms, soles and nail beds; multiple lesions in one zone suggest acral disease and favour surgical excision","Sun-exposed face, scalp and forearms; multiple lesions in one zone suggest a lower risk and favour observation alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the distribution drives the treatment choice. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What does the deck say you should do before assigning a diagnosis to a skin lesion?","choices":["Assign the most likely diagnosis first, then look for the features that confirm it — colour, texture, border, size and evolution — and discard those that do not fit","Photograph the lesion and defer all description — type, colour, texture, border, size, distribution and evolution — until the dermatology referral has been accepted","Biopsy every pigmented lesion before any description of type, colour, texture, border or evolution, since morphology is unreliable in premalignant disease","Characterise it systematically — primary lesion type, colour, surface texture, border definition, size, distribution, palpability, ulceration, bleeding, induration and temporal evolution"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — describe before naming. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What proportion of patients develop a second basal cell carcinoma, and what does that mandate?","choices":["Up to 50%, which makes examination every five years sufficient","Under 1%, which makes routine surveillance unnecessary after excision","Up to 50%, which makes at least annual full-skin examination mandatory","Up to 5%, which makes examination every five years sufficient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the second-primary risk is what drives surveillance. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Approximately what proportion of actinic keratosis lesions progresses to squamous cell carcinoma each year?","choices":["About 1 in 10 lesions per year","About 1 in 100 lesions per year","About 1 in 100,000 lesions per year","About 1 in 1,000 lesions per year"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — and cumulative field risk matters more than any single lesion. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which two clinical clues does the deck give for suspecting cutaneous T-cell lymphoma in a chronic eruption?","choices":["Itch that is entirely absent even in extensive disease, and complete sparing of the hair follicles","Sharp demarcation with thick silvery scale, and rapid complete response to a topical corticosteroid","Annular plaques with progressive central clearing, and a positive potassium hydroxide preparation from the border","Itch out of proportion to the apparent inflammatory activity, and follicular involvement with hair loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — folliculotropism discriminates from routine eczema or psoriasis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What warning does the deck give about oedema in Kaposi sarcoma?","choices":["Marked oedema occurs only in the classic form of the disease, and its severity can therefore be used to distinguish classic from epidemic Kaposi sarcoma","Marked oedema may occur even with few or no visible skin lesions, so oedema severity must not be used to gauge disease burden","Marked oedema occurs only in advanced disease with many visible lesions, so its severity reliably indicates the cutaneous disease burden","Marked oedema is never a feature of Kaposi sarcoma at any stage, and its presence should prompt a search for venous thrombosis instead"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the skin can look almost clear while disease is extensive. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What causes Kaposi sarcoma, and which cells does it arise from?","choices":["Human papillomavirus combined with a weakened immune system, arising in the keratinocytes of the epidermis","Ultraviolet damage combined with fair skin, arising in the basal cells of the epidermis","Malignant T cells migrating into the skin over years, arising in the lymphoid tissue","Human herpesvirus 8 combined with a weakened immune system, arising in the cells lining blood and lymph vessels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the virus alone is not sufficient. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which indications does the deck give for Mohs micrographic surgery in squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Only recurrent tumours and only after two failed courses of topical therapy, since site, size and immune status do not alter the surgical choice","High-risk sites (lips, temples, ears, nose, genitalia), recurrent tumours, aggressive histology with perineural or perivascular invasion, lesions over 1 cm on the face or over 2 cm on trunk or extremities, immunosuppression, tumours within scars, and genetic disease-associated tumours","High-risk sites (lips, temples, ears, nose, genitalia), recurrent tumours, aggressive histology with perineural or perivascular invasion, lesions over 2 cm on the face or over 1 cm on trunk or extremities, immunosuppression, and tumours within scars","Any lesion on the trunk, any lesion in a patient over 65, any lesion present more than a year, any pigmented lesion, and any lesion the patient finds unsightly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — note the two different size thresholds by site. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What self-examination does the deck ask melanoma patients to perform, and which sites must be included?","choices":["Monthly self-examination using the recognition mnemonic and ugly-duckling principles, including the scalp, back, palms, soles and nails","Annual self-examination using the recognition mnemonic alone, limited to the sun-exposed skin of the face, arms and upper chest","Monthly self-examination of the trunk and limbs only, since acral, nail and scalp melanoma are too rare to warrant routine checking","No self-examination at all, since surveillance of the skin, scalp, nails and acral sites is entirely the specialist team's responsibility"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the acral and scalp sites are easy to miss. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What is the classic triad of a glomus tumour?","choices":["A verrucous periungual plaque with subungual hyperkeratosis, onycholysis and painless bleeding","A longitudinal pigmented band with proximal widening, blurred borders and nail splitting","A rapidly growing friable bleeding papule with an epithelial collarette","A small red-blue subungual focus with severe paroxysmal pain, exquisite point tenderness, and cold sensitivity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the nail may look nearly normal. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"What is the most important distinction to make when assessing an actinic keratosis, and what raises that concern?","choices":["Early squamous cell carcinoma or carcinoma in situ — raised when a lesion is thick, indurated, ulcerated, enlarging, painful, bleeding, persistent or recurrent","Seborrheic keratosis — raised when a lesion is thick, waxy, stuck-on, sharply demarcated, enlarging, persistent or recurrent","Superficial basal cell carcinoma — raised when a lesion is pearly with rolled borders, visible telangiectasias, ulceration, bleeding or slow enlargement","Solar lentigo — raised when a lesion is uniformly pigmented, flat, non-indurated, painless and stable over many years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that list is the biopsy trigger. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the initial diagnostic test for a lesion suspicious for melanoma?","choices":["Serum lactate dehydrogenase and whole-body imaging","Biopsy or excision to confirm the diagnosis","Sentinel lymph node biopsy before any skin sampling","Dermoscopy alone, with biopsy reserved for lesions that change"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — tissue is required. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What metastatic rate does the deck give for actinically induced squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["An estimated 0.1 to 0.5%","An estimated 15 to 20%","An estimated 3 to 7%","An estimated 30 to 70%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — substantially higher with high-risk features. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which basal cell carcinoma subtype is scar-like and carries the highest risk of subclinical spread?","choices":["Nodular — a pearly papule with central erosion whose extension beyond the visible segment is clinically subtle","Pigmented — a stippled lesion whose extension beyond the visible segment is clinically subtle","Morpheaform or sclerosing — a scar-like or ivory-white lesion whose extension beyond the visible pink segment is clinically subtle","Superficial — a reddish shiny scaly thin plaque whose extension beyond the visible segment is clinically subtle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the invisible extension is what makes it high-risk. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What do the letters of the melanoma recognition mnemonic stand for?","choices":["Asymmetry of the halves; Bleeding from the surface; Crusting over the lesion; Diameter greater than 6 mm; Erythema surrounding the lesion","Age over 50 at onset; Border irregularity; Colour variegation; Depth greater than 1 mm; Evolution — change in the lesion","Asymmetry; Border irregular, notched or poorly defined; Colour variegation; Diameter greater than 6 cm; Evolution — change in the lesion","Asymmetry; Border irregular, notched or poorly defined; Colour variegation; Diameter greater than 6 mm; Evolution — change in the lesion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — though smaller lesions can still be melanoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which warning patterns does the deck give for basal cell carcinoma, and where do they commonly appear?","choices":["A violaceous macule, a purple plaque, or an oral lesion — commonly on the hard palate and lower limbs","A pearly papule, an erythematous patch larger than 6 mm, or a non-healing ulcer — commonly on the face, trunk or lower legs","A sandpaper-textured papule, a pigmented macule larger than 6 mm, or a warty nodule — commonly on the scalp, ears and dorsal hands","A pearly papule, an erythematous patch larger than 6 mm, or a non-healing ulcer — commonly on the palms, soles and nail units"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — three patterns, three common sites. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What follow-up schedule does the deck specify after squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["At least annual skin AND lymph-node examination, at closer intervals for high-risk or immunosuppressed patients","At least annual skin examination alone, with lymph nodes assessed only once metastasis is suspected clinically","Examination every five years, at closer intervals only for patients who have had more than ten tumours","No scheduled follow-up once the lesion has been completely excised with clear histologic margins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the node examination is part of the routine, not an extra. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which findings does the deck list as urgent referral triggers in the nail unit?","choices":["Only a subungual mass confirmed on imaging, since clinical features such as pigment, widening, dystrophy or bleeding cannot themselves indicate urgency","New or changing single-digit longitudinal melanonychia, proximal widening, Hutchinson sign, or nail dystrophy with pigment","Longitudinal pigmented bands present in several nails since childhood, unchanged in width, colour, thickness and spacing over many years, with no nail dystrophy","Any nail change at all in a patient over 65, and any nail that has been abnormal for more than a month, whatever the appearance or the number of digits involved"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the list also includes an unexplained subungual mass, persistent ulceration or bleeding, and a chronic wart or infection that fails appropriate therapy. That failed-wart history is the one most easily dismissed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"When is field-directed therapy indicated for actinic keratosis, and which options does the deck list as most effective?","choices":["When the patient is immunosuppressed or transplanted; oral acitretin, systemic methotrexate, photodynamic therapy, and methotrexate plus calcipotriene as a possible benefit","When the lesion has recurred twice after cryotherapy; radiation therapy, hedgehog pathway inhibitors, programmed death 1 blockade, and cetuximab as a possible benefit","When multiple lesions are present in one anatomic region, reflecting field cancerization risk; topical fluorouracil, imiquimod, photodynamic therapy, and fluorouracil plus calcipotriene as a possible benefit","When a single lesion has clear borders and has not recurred; liquid nitrogen cryotherapy, curettage and electrodesiccation, Mohs micrographic surgery, and excision with 5 mm margins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — four options, the last flagged as possible rather than established. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is the overall prognosis of basal cell carcinoma, and where does its morbidity come from?","choices":["Usually slow-growing and highly curable when treated early; morbidity comes from local destruction, recurrence, delayed diagnosis and anatomically complex sites, with metastatic disease rare but serious","Usually rapidly growing and rarely curable even when treated early; morbidity comes chiefly from early distant metastasis to lung and liver, with local destruction uncommon","Usually slow-growing and highly curable when treated early; morbidity comes chiefly from nodal metastasis, which occurs in 3 to 7% of cases, rather than from local destruction","Usually slow-growing but uniformly fatal without systemic therapy; morbidity comes from delayed hedgehog inhibitor treatment rather than from local destruction or recurrence"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the danger is local destruction rather than spread. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What patient education does the deck give for actinic keratosis?","choices":["Avoid all sun exposure permanently rather than using broad-spectrum protection or protective clothing; stop field therapy at the first sign of erythema or crusting; attend annual review regardless of any change in a lesion","No specific education is needed once the lesions have been treated successfully, since the surrounding field carries no further risk and reactions to therapy are not expected","Daily broad-spectrum sun protection and protective clothing; expect local treatment reactions such as erythema and crusting and adhere to the field therapy duration; seek prompt review of any non-healing or changing lesion","Sun protection in the summer months only and no protective clothing; expect no local reaction to field treatment; return only if an entirely new lesion appears"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — warning about the expected reaction protects adherence. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How is squamous cell carcinoma in situ managed when there are no high-risk features?","choices":["Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy alone, with no role for topical therapy, curettage or electrodesiccation","Imiquimod, topical fluorouracil, or curettage and electrodesiccation in appropriately selected disease","Surgical excision or Mohs micrographic surgery in every case, whatever the features and whatever the site","Programmed death 1 blockade or cetuximab in every case, whatever the features and whatever the site"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — three options for in situ disease without high-risk features. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the first priority in acquired immunodeficiency syndrome-associated Kaposi sarcoma?","choices":["Reduce immunosuppressive medication doses — dose reduction is the cornerstone of treatment for epidemic disease","Begin or optimise antiretroviral therapy — immune restoration is the cornerstone of treatment for epidemic disease","Begin liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel — systemic chemotherapy is the cornerstone of treatment for epidemic disease","Begin intralesional vinblastine and radiation — local palliation is the cornerstone of treatment for epidemic disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — antiretroviral therapy comes before local measures. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"}]};
